242-**Reading** (`readTrustRecord(path)`) yields one of:
243-
244-| file state | result |
245-|---|---|
246-| absent (`ENOENT`) | `{kind:'current', approved:[]}` |
247-| JSON object, `version` is the number `2` | `{kind:'current', approved}` |
248-| JSON object, `version` is the number `1` | `{kind:'legacy'}` |
249-| anything else | error |
250-
251-- For version 2: `approved` missing or `null` reads as `[]`; otherwise it
252-  must be an array of strings, else error. Unknown top-level members are
253-  ignored on read (Go parity) and dropped on the next write.
254-- For version 1 (written by the Go implementation): nothing else in the
255-  file is read or validated. Its hashes are never compared: the Go hash
256-  serialization is not reproduced (ADR-0003).
257-- Errors: unreadable file -> `read trust record: <cause>`; invalid JSON or
258-  a non-object or wrongly typed `approved` -> `read trust record <path>:
259-  <cause>`; `version` missing or any value other than 1 or 2 -> `trust
260-  record <path> has unsupported version <v>`. Errors are never treated as
261-  "not approved"; they propagate (2.8).
